   "source": [
    "# 1.Project introduction\n",
    "Chicago records more than 100,000 reported traffic crashes a year. Most cause only property damage, but a small share cause fatal or incapacitating injuries. Our project asks: what makes a Chicago traffic crash severe, and where and when are the most dangerous crashes? Understanding which conditions are linked to the most serious crashes can help the city decide where to focus safety improvements such as lighting, speed limits, and enforcement.\n",
    "\n",
    "Chicago records more than 100,000 reported traffic crashes a year. Most cause only property damage, but a small share cause fatal or incapacitating injuries. Our project asks: what makes a Chicago traffic crash severe, and where and when are the most dangerous crashes? Understanding which conditions are linked to the most serious crashes can help the city decide where to focus safety improvements such as lighting, speed limits, and enforcement.\n",
    "\n",
    "Data: We use the Traffic Crashes, Crashes dataset from the Chicago Data Portal, which contains reported crashes on Chicago city streets handled by the Chicago Police Department. After cleaning, we have 968,596 crashes from 2018 through October 2026, with details on the time, weather, lighting, road surface, posted speed limit, crash type, hit and run status, location, and injury outcome. We define a crash as severe if it involved a fatal or incapacitating injury. About 1.8 percent of crashes are severe, which makes severe crashes rare and harder to study.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our hypotheses:\n",
    "\n",
    "H1: Higher speed limits lead to more severe crashes.\n",
    "\n",
    "H2: Darkness leads to more severe crashes.\n",
    "\n",
    "H3: Bad weather raises crash counts but not severity.\n",
    "\n",
    "H4: Hit and run crashes are more common at night.\n",
    "\n",
    "So far we have tested H1 and H2 with chi square tests and visualizations, tested H4 with an hour of day analysis, and tested both sides of H3. The severity side came from the chi square tests, and for the crash count side we joined daily Chicago weather to daily crash counts and found that rain and snow days had about 4 to 5 percent more crashes than dry days."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 2. Changes since the proposal\n",
    "Our main question and four hypotheses have not changed since the proposal. Our scope has changed in the following ways.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our main question and four hypotheses have not changed since the proposal. Our scope has changed in the following ways.\n",
    "\n",
    "Parts removed from our plan:\n",
    "\n",
    "So far we have used only the Crashes table. We have not used the People or Vehicles tables, and we treat these as optional extras if time allows. We also replaced NOAA as our weather source (see below). Teammates: confirm whether anything else was dropped, or whether any of the planned analyses changed.\n",
    "\n",
    "Parts added to our plan:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. We limited the data to 2018 onward. Years before 2018 have far fewer records, which suggests incomplete reporting, and including them would have distorted any comparison across years.\n",
    "2. We defined a severe crash as one with a fatal or incapacitating injury, so we could study the most serious outcomes and not just any injury.\n",
    "3. We added a separate speed limit analysis (Viz 1b) to test H1 directly.\n",
    "4. We added a hit and run analysis by hour of day (Viz 5) to test H4.\n",
    "5. We decided to leave the injury, damage, and contributory cause columns out of any prediction model, because those are only known after the crash and would make a model look better than it really is.\n",
    "6. We decided to compare severity as a rate (the share of crashes that are severe) and not as raw counts, because the data has no traffic volume information.\n",
    "7. We added daily weather data (temperature, rain, and snow) from Open-Meteo for Chicago O’Hare, which we used in place of NOAA because it is free and needs no ordering. We joined it to daily crash counts to test the crash count side of H3.\n",
    "8. We added a daily crash forecasting model (ML analysis 2) that predicts how many crashes happen each day from weather and calendar features, compared against a 7-day average baseline.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Teammates: add anything new from your part, such as NOAA weather data, new features, or a new analysis.**\n",
    "\n",
    "Why the scope changed:\n",
    "\n",
    "Cleaning and exploring a dataset of about 1.1 million rows took more time than we expected, so the machine learning analyses were finished later than planned. We also switched from NOAA to Open-Meteo for weather data, since it was free and available right away and let us start the weather analysis without waiting on a data order. **Teammates: add any other reasons your work changed.**"
