    "## 4. Exploratory data analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "Show what the data looks like and note anything interesting or odd. Suggested pieces: crashes per year, crashes by hour of day and day of week, the share of severe crashes, and anything surprising.\n",
    "\n",
    "Stats tests (Salwa Majeed): We ran chi square tests of severity against lighting, speed limit, and weather. All three were statistically significant, but the links were weak (Cramer's V of 0.035 for lighting, 0.034 for speed limit, and 0.011 for weather). Lighting and speed limit had the strongest links, and weather had the weakest. Each factor on its own explains only a small part of severity.\n",
    "\n",
    "Weather results (Salwa Majeed): Severe crash rates were similar across most weather conditions (about 1.6 to 2.2 percent). Rain was slightly higher than clear weather (2.16 versus 1.89 percent), and snow was lower (1.24 percent). This is consistent with the severity part of H3, but H3 also says bad weather raises the number of crashes, which will be tested separately in the weather and forecasting analysis. Categories with fewer than about 1,000 crashes were not used for conclusions because their rates are unreliable.\n",
    "**Add more here**"
   ]
  },
