   "source": [
    "Viz 3 (Yaswin Chebrolu): We tested H3, that bad weather raises crash counts but not severity. We thought this was interesting because weather is the one factor in our project that nobody can change, so it shows whether severity is mostly about conditions people can fix (lighting, speed limits) or about luck. Severe crash rates ranged from 1.24 percent in snow to 2.40 percent in blowing snow, around an overall rate of 1.79 percent. Bad weather did bring more crashes per day: rain days averaged about 310 crashes and snow days about 307, compared with about 295 on dry days, an increase of roughly 4 to 5 percent. Severity did not follow the same pattern. Snow was below the overall severe rate, and rain was only slightly above it. This supports H3: bad weather nudges up how many crashes happen but does not consistently make them worse. Limitations: weather comes from one station (O’Hare) for the whole city, and rare conditions like blowing snow have few crashes, so their rates are less reliable."
   ]
