   "source": [
    "Viz 5 (Salwa): We tested H4, that hit and run crashes are more common at night. We thought this was an interesting hypothesis because a hit and run is both a safety problem and an accountability problem, and knowing when they cluster could help target enforcement and lighting. Hit and run crashes made up 41.95 percent of crashes between 8 pm and 5:59 am, compared with 27.34 percent during the day. The share rises through the evening, peaks at about 49 percent around 3 am, and falls quickly after 5 am to a low of about 26 percent in the early afternoon. A chi square test confirmed the difference is statistically significant (chi square of about 16,895, p value far below 0.05), and the gap of about 15 percentage points is large, not just a product of the big sample size. This supports H4. Limitations: this measures the share of crashes that were hit and run at each hour, not the number of hit and run crashes, since there are far fewer crashes overall late at night. Also, crashes with a blank hit and run field were counted as not hit and run, so the true shares may be slightly higher."
   ]
