    "ML analysis 2 (Yaswin Chebrolu)\n",
    "\n",
    "Question: Can we forecast daily crash counts using weather and calendar features?\n",
    "\n",
    "Features used: daily mean temperature, rainfall, snowfall, day of week, and month. One version also used the average crashes over the previous 7 days. Daily weather came from Open-Meteo for Chicago O’Hare.\n",
    "\n",
    "Baselines: (1) predict the average of the previous 7 days, and (2) predict the overall training average every day.\n",
    "\n",
    "Models: linear regression and random forest. We trained on 2018 through 2025 and tested on 2026, so the model never sees the future. The test year has about 306 crashes per day on average.\n",
    "\n",
    "Results (mean absolute error, crashes per day, lower is better):\n",
    "\n",
    "Model: Error\n",
    "Baseline: previous 7-day average: 36.1\n",
    "Random forest (weather + calendar): 30.2\n",
    "Random forest (weather + calendar + previous 7-day average): 29.9\n",
    "\n",
    "Interpretation: Our best model was off by about 30 crashes per day, compared with 36 for the 7-day baseline, so weather and calendar information beats simply guessing from recent days. Adding the recent crash level only improved the error by about 0.3 crashes a day, so weather and the calendar carry most of the useful signal. The model still misses by roughly 10 percent of a typical day, so it is useful but not precise. One limitation is that the model uses actual weather rather than a forecast, and the calendar features are included alongside weather, so we can’t say how much of the improvement comes from weather alone.",
    "#### Baseline Model: Majorty Class Classifier \n",
    "Majority Class is non-severe crashes so it always predicts non-severe"
